  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1141 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nTonight through Thursday...\n\nTwo lines of storms developed this evening as a cold front drifted \nthrough the region. Storms generally struggled to develop beyond sub-\nsevere limits through the evening hours, with stronger storms \ninitiating to our east in Des Moines CWA. \n\nThe spotlight will shift to hydro concerns through the overnight \nhours, despite the lack of deep updraft development. The 00Z \nsounding depicted a PWAT of 2.03\", which is well above the \nclimatological max of 1.81. Training has already begun with the SW \nto NE oriented line. Radar estimates and mesonet reports indicate \namounts up to 1-2\" have fallen, as of 11 PM. Additional amounts up \nto 1-3\" will be possible through the overnight hours, as the \nincreasing LLJ noses into the boundary reigniting convection. \n\nA surface high will sink south behind the front, ushering precip \nsoutheast of the forecast area by late Wednesday afternoon. \nnoticeably cooler temperatures will finally settle into the region, \nwith highs topping out in the upper 70s and low 80s. Similar \nconditions can be expected again on Thursday. \n\nFriday and Beyond...\n\nSubtle ridging will boost temperatures back into the 80s on Friday, \nwith breezy southerly winds. The warmup will be short-lived, \nthankfully, as a cold front is progged to move in from the northwest \nFriday evening. Given the late afternoon/evening expected timing of \nthe frontal passage, sufficient instability will likely be in place \nfor deep moist convection. Additionally, increasing layer shear with \nan approaching shortwave will likely aide severe storm potential. As \nsuch, the SPC has highlighted much of the forecast area in a 15% \nDay4 severe outlook. \n\nTemperatures dive into the 70s Saturday, with a few locations only \nreaching the upper 60s by Sunday, finally giving us a hint of Fall. \nLooking further ahead, long-range guidance continues to hint at \nbringing a mid-level low across the northern Plains late next \nweekend into early next week. This system is expected to bring \nanother round of 40-60% PoPs as well as a push of CAA into the \nregion, keeping temperatures in the 70s early next week.",
